00:14:31.584 --> 00:14:35.084
Typically with these we always
want to round them to sig figs and

00:14:35.084 --> 00:14:36.694
put them in scientific notation.

00:14:37.204 --> 00:14:41.155
So we can see that, hey, we're going
to be moving this decimal five times,

00:14:41.805 --> 00:14:47.105
um, and we want to round it to three
sig figs because we started with three

00:14:47.105 --> 00:14:49.185
sig figs at the very, very beginning.

00:14:49.935 --> 00:14:54.924
So what are we going to have in the end
is negative 5, 000, or sorry, negative 5.

00:14:54.925 --> 00:15:02.935
47 times 10 to the 5th kilojoules is
your final answer for this problem.

00:15:04.125 --> 00:15:06.864
So this is how you can do, how
you can find the amount of energy.

00:15:06.915 --> 00:15:11.175
Now our last example is going to
be find how the amount of mass

00:15:11.205 --> 00:15:15.285
that was either created or used
to make this amount of energy.

00:15:15.285 --> 00:15:16.395
So we're going to do the backwards.

00:15:16.815 --> 00:15:18.485
Let's take a look at our last problem.

00:15:18.575 --> 00:15:20.185
Let's practice this problem.

00:15:20.685 --> 00:15:26.165
So what we have is diboron trioxide,
which is a solid formed by the reaction

00:15:26.255 --> 00:15:30.044
of igniting boron powder in oxygen gas.

00:15:30.735 --> 00:15:34.384
And what it's telling you is that
we also have a delta H reaction

00:15:34.535 --> 00:15:36.829
of negative 1273 kilojoules.

00:15:37.509 --> 00:15:42.910
Now, and it's asking, how much
diboron trioxide did you start with

00:15:43.360 --> 00:15:46.680
if 1690 kilojoules is released?

00:15:47.219 --> 00:15:51.969
Assume di, assume a diboron
trioxide molar mass of 69.

00:15:52.039 --> 00:15:53.399
62 grams per mole.

00:15:54.119 --> 00:15:59.209
Okay, so these types of problems are
very, very difficult because we weren't

00:15:59.250 --> 00:16:01.150
given the actual chemical equation.

00:16:01.160 --> 00:16:03.630
We have to read this very, very carefully.

00:16:04.180 --> 00:16:05.170
When doing it.

00:16:05.530 --> 00:16:08.410
So the first thing that we are
going to do is that we are going

00:16:08.410 --> 00:16:11.390
to balance our chemical equation.

00:16:11.899 --> 00:16:19.129
Now what it says is that D boron trioxide,
so dye is two, so it's gonna be B two.

00:16:19.189 --> 00:16:22.099
Trioxide is three, so B 2 0 3.

00:16:22.610 --> 00:16:25.280
And it's a solid and it says it's formed.

00:16:25.400 --> 00:16:25.699
Okay.

00:16:25.699 --> 00:16:29.241
So formed means that it was
created or that it is a.

00:16:30.110 --> 00:16:31.330
product in this case.

00:16:31.760 --> 00:16:33.819
So we're going to write that on
the right hand side of the hour,

00:16:33.890 --> 00:16:35.740
the, the, the arrow in this case.

00:16:36.489 --> 00:16:41.480
Now it says we're going to ignite
boron powder with oxygen gas.

00:16:41.949 --> 00:16:45.640
Boron is a solid, so that
just is, and it's a powder, so

00:16:45.640 --> 00:16:46.629
that's going to be by itself.

00:16:47.345 --> 00:16:51.945
Oxygen is a diatomic gas,
so we have that as well.

00:16:52.694 --> 00:16:56.444
Now when we are balancing this,
we have to make sure that we

00:16:56.444 --> 00:16:57.764
are using our coefficients.

00:16:58.265 --> 00:17:01.494
So the first tip and trick I
always say is let's even the odds.

00:17:01.844 --> 00:17:05.915
You can see that we have three
oxygens on the right, meaning

00:17:05.915 --> 00:17:07.685
it's odd, so let's even it.

00:17:08.405 --> 00:17:13.605
Uh, so we're going to add a two in front
of that to make six, which is even number.

00:17:14.175 --> 00:17:17.185
I'll add a three in front of the
oxygen on the left hand side.

00:17:17.444 --> 00:17:21.354
We evened the odds in this case,
and now what we can do is balance

00:17:21.355 --> 00:17:25.754
our borons, and we see that we have
four and four on the left and right.

00:17:26.615 --> 00:17:30.535
Now our next part of information
that was given to you was the delta

00:17:30.615 --> 00:17:35.145
H of the reaction, which as we can
kind of see if we pull this out.

00:17:35.555 --> 00:17:40.625
It was negative 1273 kilojoules,
which means that it's a product.

00:17:40.995 --> 00:17:46.615
So we can also relate that to, um,
exactly being that it's talking

00:17:46.615 --> 00:17:52.065
about diboron trioxide, we can
relate it to that value as well.

00:17:52.304 --> 00:17:55.465
We can relate it to the number
of moles that is in the problem.

00:17:55.465 --> 00:17:59.745
Being that it asks, okay, if we have
diboron trioxide, how much is there?

00:18:00.055 --> 00:18:01.705
And we release this much energy.

00:18:03.335 --> 00:18:06.925
What we are going to do is we are
going to define our conversion

00:18:06.925 --> 00:18:09.505
term just like we did before.

00:18:10.035 --> 00:18:16.415
And being that we have negative 1273
kilojoules, that's for every mole.

00:18:16.584 --> 00:18:18.445
And, well, which mole are we going to use?

00:18:18.495 --> 00:18:21.085
Well, the problem asks for B2O3.

00:18:21.715 --> 00:18:26.765
So this is going to be equivalent
to for every two moles of B2O3.

00:18:28.399 --> 00:18:31.439
And you can write this conversion term
for all the other values too, but they're

00:18:31.439 --> 00:18:34.330
not, um, not important for this problem.

00:18:35.149 --> 00:18:39.019
So when it comes to solving it,
we were given another value as

00:18:39.020 --> 00:18:45.129
well, which was that you had
negative 1690 kilojoules of energy.

00:18:45.445 --> 00:18:46.825
that was released.

00:18:47.295 --> 00:18:49.025
So, which is a given term, right?

00:18:49.025 --> 00:18:52.685
You want to start with that because
it's by itself, it's not a fraction.

00:18:53.225 --> 00:18:59.384
Um, versus the other value that we have,
which is two moles of B2O3 is equivalent

00:18:59.394 --> 00:19:03.175
to that six, that 12, 973 kilojoules.

00:19:04.265 --> 00:19:06.744
This term can be flipped
one way or another.

00:19:06.744 --> 00:19:09.044
So you don't know which one to start with.

00:19:09.445 --> 00:19:13.005
If you choose wrongly, your units are
going to be flipped and it could lead

00:19:13.005 --> 00:19:15.005
to confusion when you're at the end.

00:19:15.475 --> 00:19:19.034
But the value that's on the left
hand side over here, that can

00:19:19.034 --> 00:19:20.455
only, you can't flip it, right?

00:19:20.465 --> 00:19:22.234
It's, it's easy the way it is.

00:19:22.235 --> 00:19:23.264
We can just start with that.

00:19:23.795 --> 00:19:26.925
So we're going to start there and we need
to use the balanced chemical equation.

00:19:27.415 --> 00:19:32.025
So what we're going to do is
essentially cancel our units.

00:19:32.375 --> 00:19:34.435
So we'll start with negative 1270.

00:19:35.014 --> 00:19:38.104
And now we're, we need
to cancel out kilojoules.

00:19:38.114 --> 00:19:39.804
So how can we exactly.

00:19:40.405 --> 00:19:43.385
do that, well, we need to
have kilojoules on the bottom.

00:19:44.035 --> 00:19:48.555
So what we can use is this term
on the top right here, and kind

00:19:48.555 --> 00:19:50.915
of plug this value in like so.

00:19:51.555 --> 00:19:55.415
And now what we can see is,
hey, your kilojoules cancelled.

00:19:55.865 --> 00:19:56.515
Fantastic.

00:19:56.615 --> 00:20:00.375
That is what we want when we
are looking at these problems.

00:20:01.195 --> 00:20:05.605
Now we have our units in
moles, so how can we get grams?

00:20:06.054 --> 00:20:10.885
is we need to use the molar mass of
B2O3, which was given in the problem.

00:20:11.435 --> 00:20:14.305
So earlier that said it was 69.

00:20:14.415 --> 00:20:17.145
62 grams per mole.

00:20:17.534 --> 00:20:21.485
So let's kind of write that up here
so we can write it in correctly.

00:20:22.175 --> 00:20:23.685
So we had 69.

00:20:23.955 --> 00:20:31.035
62 grams and that's equivalent
for every one mole of B2O3.

00:20:31.355 --> 00:20:34.615
And now we can see our, once again,
our units are doing what they're

00:20:34.615 --> 00:20:36.124
supposed to do, which is cancel.

00:20:36.664 --> 00:20:42.524
And now we're left only with grams
of B2O3, which is what we want.

00:20:42.904 --> 00:20:47.010
So when we are solving this, we're
going to multiply everything on top and

00:20:47.010 --> 00:20:49.100
then divide by what's on the bottom.

00:20:49.190 --> 00:20:54.470
So we'll take negative 1690,
times that by 2, times that by 69.

00:20:54.470 --> 00:21:00.270
62, and then divide by negative 1273.

00:21:00.920 --> 00:21:04.210
And what we are going
to get, you'll get 184.

00:21:04.269 --> 00:21:10.460
85 grams of B2O3, but we need to round
it to the correct number of sig figs.

00:21:10.810 --> 00:21:16.095
We have 4 in this It's always being
based off what was given in the problem.

00:21:16.395 --> 00:21:20.014
So as you can see, the number we started
with over here on the left is four.

00:21:20.695 --> 00:21:24.294
So we'll round this slightly to 184.

00:21:24.344 --> 00:21:27.705
9 grams of B2O3.

00:21:28.255 --> 00:21:31.485
And that's how you solve These
are the boat the two types of

00:21:31.485 --> 00:21:33.105
problems you can see with enthalpy.

00:21:33.595 --> 00:21:35.125
It doesn't get any crazier than that.

